(g/mol, density = 3.95 g/cm^3\). Predict whether the oxide layer is protective.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Stoichiometric Reaction:) Tj
T*
(2 Al + 1.5 O2 -> Al2O3.) Tj
T*
(2 moles of Al metal produce 1 mole of Al2O3 oxide.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Volume of Al2O3 formed \(V_oxide\):) Tj
T*
(V_oxide = Molar Mass / Density = 101.96 g / 3.95 g/cm^3 = 25.81 cm^3 per mol of Al2O3.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 3: Volume of Al consumed \(V_metal\):) Tj
T*
(2 moles of Al consumed = 2 * \(26.98 g / 2.70 g/cm^3\) = 2 * \(9.99 cm^3\) = 19.99 cm^3.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 4: Compute Pilling-Bedworth Ratio \(PB\):) Tj
T*
(PB Ratio = V_oxide / V_metal = 25.81 cm^3 / 19.99 cm^3 = 1.29.) Tj
T*
(Conclusion: Since the PB ratio \(1.29\) falls in the optimal range between 1.0 and 2.0,) Tj
T*
(the Al2O3 film is continuous, non-porous, and tightly adherent, conferring excellent) Tj
T*
(corrosion passivation.) Tj
