T*
(* Q1: State the Pilling-Bedworth rule and explain its significance in determining the) Tj
T*
(protective nature of oxide films on metals.) Tj
T*
(Answer: The Pilling-Bedworth \(PB\) rule evaluates whether atmospheric oxidation forms a) Tj
T*
(protective or non-protective oxide layer. PB Ratio is defined as: PB = \(Volume of metal) Tj
T*
(oxide formed\) / \(Volume of metal consumed\) = [ M_oxide * rho_metal ] / [ n * A_metal *) Tj
T*
(rho_oxide ]. \(1\) If PB < 1 \(Alkali/alkaline-earth metals like Na, Mg, Ca\): The volume of) Tj
T*
(oxide formed is smaller than the metal consumed. The film cannot cover the surface) Tj
T*
(completely, developing porous microscopic fissures that allow oxygen to continuously) Tj
T*
(penetrate and consume the substrate. \(2\) If 1 <= PB <= 2 \(Al, Cr, Ni, Ti\): The oxide) Tj
T*
(volume exceeds metal volume just enough to pack densely under light compressive stress.) Tj
T*
(The film forms a continuous, non-porous, self-passivating diffusion barrier halting) Tj
T*
(further oxidation. \(3\) If PB > 2 \(Fe\): Excessive compressive stresses cause buckling,) Tj
T*
(cracking, and spalling of the oxide coat, re-exposing fresh metal.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain the mechanism of differential aeration corrosion with a labeled diagram) Tj
T*
(description of pitting corrosion.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Differential aeration corrosion occurs when a metallic structure is exposed to) Tj
T*
(non-uniform concentrations of dissolved oxygen. The fundamental electrochemical rule:) Tj
T*
(REGIONS EXPOSED TO LOWER OXYGEN BECOME ANODIC AND CORRODE; REGIONS EXPOSED TO HIGHER) Tj
T*
(OXYGEN BECOME CATHODIC AND ARE PROTECTED. Mechanism in Pitting: When a particle of dirt) Tj
T*
(or an imperfect coating covers a localized spot on a steel plate, oxygen diffusion to) Tj
T*
(the metal underneath is restricted. The shielded area under the drop/pit becomes anodic:) Tj
T*
(Fe -> Fe2+ + 2 e-. The surrounding unshielded surface with abundant oxygen acts as) Tj
T*
(cathode: O2 + 2 H2O + 4 e- -> 4 OH-. The area ratio of small anode to massive cathode) Tj
T*
(concentrates current density, driving rapid, intense localized digging into the plate,) Tj
T*
(forming deep microscopic pits that cause sudden structural failure.) Tj
T*
